Binary files
description: opaque bytes, read lazily, decoded by format
- >
  `file/binary` is opaque bytes — an image, a pdf, a djvu, an unknown or large blob: read
  lazily, never parsed as yamlover data. What the bytes *mean* is the format's job:
  `image/png` marks an image, `application/pdf` a document, `int32/le` a four-byte integer.
- >
  So even a SCALAR may live as raw bytes: a `file/binary` member whose `.yo/meta.yo` entry
  gives the decoding — `type: binary` with `format: int32/le` turns a four-byte file into an
  integer; `image/png` keeps an image an image. The data is the on-disk file; the metadata says
  how to read it — the instance and its description never trade places
  ([metadata first](::language:principles:metadata-first)).
